State when concentrations approximate activities and when they do not (@gc-activities: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F", #source "../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #general-chemistry "Mass-charge-and-speciation-balances.lingua#r_1PWQSP02CDTPRX1N5NPXTVJJAR", #water-and-sanitation "../../../../ergon/utilities/water-and-sanitation/foundations/Concentrations-species-and-analytical-bases-in-water.lingua#r_35TB3P0H9QMBS17JF52YF9W74Z") { r_7CCX4WJ7S62EAWJWFH2RHP7GXD
**Read:** [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), activity, composition, and equilibrium conventions; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 6 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), nonideal solution and advanced acid/base calculation material.

Thermodynamic equilibrium expressions use dimensionless activities under identified standard-state conventions. A concentration model might write a_i = γ_i c_i/c°, while a molality-based convention uses its own standard and coefficient. For gases a fugacity/standard-fugacity ratio can be appropriate. Write the selected relation rather than attaching units to a logarithm without explanation.

Activity coefficients account for departures from the selected ideal reference. Ionic strength summarizes a charge-weighted composition under a defined concentration or molality basis, such as I_m = ½Σb_i z_i². Dilute electrolyte models have assumptions and validity ranges; a convenient formula does not establish accuracy at arbitrary salinity or for every species.

A conditional concentration constant can be useful at fixed medium conditions, but changing ionic strength or composition can change its numerical value. Free-ion activity, total analytical concentration, and an operational measurement response need not coincide. Preserve that distinction when comparing textbooks, tables, and laboratory observations.

**Work:** rewrite a simple equilibrium using explicit standard quantities, derive the ideal approximation, and identify every omitted coefficient. Compute ionic strength for a defined simple electrolyte mixture and compare two compositions having different charge distributions. Use an identified dilute-solution model only within a justified range, then analyze how its assumptions affect a predicted equilibrium.

Read Harvey's full equilibrium chapter and Green Book conventions, comparing them with the first textbook's simpler equations. The aim is to understand the accuracy and meaning of a useful approximation, not to discard elementary calculations or demand a full nonideal model before any learning can begin.

